2026-10, by PLN:
  The EBADF seen in CI on this branch was not a mock socket race.
  The upload passed a numeric FileHandle descriptor to
  fs.createReadStream. After a mid-read abort the stream's close ran
  again once the in-flight read finished, and that second close threw
  EBADF: bad file descriptor, close. That is fixed on main in PR 7
  (1b13ffc): the upload reads through FileHandle.createReadStream, and
  while a read is in flight fh.close() waits for the stream's close
  rather than doing nothing. The test
  mock no longer ignores EBADF. No code from another pikvm_mcp_server
  fork was added.

2026-10, by PLN:
  The upload stream was fs.createReadStream with the numeric
  FileHandle descriptor and autoClose false. destroy() closes that
  descriptor. While a read was still in flight, the stream's close
  ran after the read returned. That second close threw
  EBADF: bad file descriptor, close. Nothing handled it, so the
  process exited. When the descriptor number had been reused, that
  close applied to some other file or socket.
  The upload now reads through FileHandle.createReadStream with
  autoClose false, start 0, and end size - 1. The stream holds one
  ref on the handle. While a read is in flight, fh.close() waits for
  the stream's close rather than doing nothing, and the descriptor
  is closed once, after the read. When the stream was never created,
  only fh.close() runs. A connection reset, an idle timeout, and a
  source read error each take that path. hashFd still uses positioned
  FileHandle.read and does not open a stream. No other path passes a
  raw fd to createReadStream.
  No code from another pikvm_mcp_server fork was added.

2026-10, by PLN:
